[s0l0!-header][h1]Pointers and Functions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

We can pass pointers as function [b]parameters[/b].
Let's look at an example:[code format="go" codeId="3334"]func change(val int) {
  val = 8
}
func change_ptr(ptr *int) {
  *ptr = 8
}

func main() {
  x := 42

  change(x)
  fmt.Println(x)

  change_ptr(&x)
  fmt.Println(x)
}[/code]
The [b]change[/b]() function takes an integer argument and changes its value.
The [b]change_ptr[/b]() function does the same using a pointer.

When you run the code, you will see that the [b]change[/b]() function did not change the value of our [b]x[/b] variable, because the argument is just a copy of its value, while the [b]change_ptr[/b]() did change the actual value of [b]x[/b], because it used its memory address as the argument.
[note]Note that we need to pass the memory address using the [b]&[/b] operator to functions that take a pointer as their argument.[/note]
